[gd_scene load_steps=3 format=3 uid="uid://windman_3d_updated_001"]

; WINDMAN, SKINNED — bead godot-test1-5u3.5, the default hero on a Skeleton3D.
; One mesh on MPFB2's `game_engine` rig, 23 bones after `build_hero.cut_fingers()`,
; built by `scripts/build_hero.py --hero windman` (the source of record;
; `assets/models/characters/PROVENANCE.md` carries the row). The ten generated
; parts this scene used to instance are retired with this bead, and so is the
; authored head — it is part of the same mesh now, built from the same MakeHuman
; human at the same scale, which is what closes the neck seam by construction.
;
; THE SCENE IS THE FLAG (`scenes/characters/teibi.tscn` said it first): the
; Skeleton3D under `Body` is what makes `hero_rig.gd` hand him to
; `hero_rig_skeleton.gd` instead of `hero_rig_limbs.gd` (bd 5u3.2), which is why
; this migration is a data change and `player_animation.gd`, `player_controller.gd`,
; `remote_avatar.gd` and `player_abilities.gd` change by zero lines.
;
; THE UID IS LOAD-BEARING and stays `uid://windman_3d_updated_001`:
; `player_controller.gd`'s CHARACTERS points at the PATH, but a changed uid makes
; Godot rewrite every reference that resolved this scene by uid, and warn while it
; does it.
;
; `Body` stays even though the mesh needs no wrapper: the landing squash writes
; Body.position.y, `capture_rest_pose()` reads Body.rotation and the caller writes
; the lean and sway there for BOTH rig kinds. The .glb instance carries NO basis —
; a SKINNED glTF is exported Y-up and placed by its own root node, so the Rx(-90)
; every UNRIGGED part scene applies would lay the hero on his face.
;
; THE FAN IS A BoneAttachment3D, AND IT IS DATA. `player_abilities.gd` has never
; looked this node up — Air Rush is a velocity impulse and a particle burst, not a
; prop animation — so the fan moving from `Body/RightArm/LowerArm/Mesh` to the
; right hand's BONE is a scene edit and nothing else.
;
;   * `use_external_skeleton`, NOT editable children, and that is the "survives a
;     re-import" half of the bead's question. Editable children would pin this
;     scene to the imported .glb's node layout — Godot rewrites an editable
;     instance's stored state on re-import and drops overrides whose nodes moved —
;     whereas an external skeleton is ONE NodePath resolved at run time, which a
;     rebuilt .glb either still answers or fails loudly on. `build_hero.py` names
;     the armature and the mesh datablock, so `Armature/Skeleton3D` is stable
;     across rebuilds.
;   * The OFFSET lives on the child, never on the attachment: BoneAttachment3D
;     overwrites its own transform with the bone's pose every frame (the
;     `CameraArm` rule in CLAUDE.md, same failure mode).
;   * The basis is MEASURED, not guessed, and it is the hand's rest basis
;     INVERTED. `hand_r` rests with x (0.5006, 0.7235, -0.4753),
;     y (0.0234, -0.5602, -0.8280), z (-0.8654, 0.4034, -0.2974) — Blender's bone
;     convention, so +Y runs from the wrist toward the fingertips, which on this
;     rest pose is 56 degrees off vertical (MakeHuman rests with a bent elbow).
;     The fan is authored trimesh Z-up with the grip at Z ~ 0 and the pinwheel at
;     -Z, and the picture we are reproducing is the one the ten-part Windman
;     shipped: blades hanging BELOW the fist with their disc facing the camera.
;     So the columns below are Godot's own axes written in the hand's frame —
;     the fan's +Z on world UP (the pinwheel therefore hangs down) and its +Y,
;     the disc's normal, on world FORWARD. Expressed in the BONE's frame, so it
;     is not a world lock: the fan turns with the hand every time the arm swings,
;     which is what shot 19 is for. Aligning the handle with the fingers instead
;     was tried first and put the disc 56 degrees edge-on to every camera.
;   * The 5.5 cm along the bone's +Y is the palm: `hand_r`'s origin is the WRIST.

